<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quiriguá</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 200–850</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quiriguá is an ancient Maya archaeological site on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/motagua-river">Motagua River</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Izabal, south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, where several Classic period trade routes met. Occupied by 200 CE, it was long a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copan">Copán</a>, with which it shares its architectural and sculptural styles. In 738 its king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kak-tiliw-chan-yopaat">K'ak' Tiliw Chan Yopaat</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waxaklajuun-ubaah-kawiil">Uaxaclajuun Ub'aah K'awiil</a> of Copán and had him sacrificed, and the city kept its independence and expanded rapidly. Its modest architecture is outweighed by its sculpture in hard red sandstone, including Stela E of 771, the tallest stela of the Maya world. Construction had halted by about 850; the site joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> World Heritage List in 1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quirigua.jpg" alt="Quiriguá" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jan Pešula · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GT_Quirigua_StelaE.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rulers raided rival cities to take captives, who were displayed, humiliated and often sacrificed, and the greatest wars could end reigns: in 738 Waxaklajuun Ub’aah K’awiil, king of Copán, was captured and executed by his former vassal, the king of <b>Quiriguá</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>…to their thrones, but war could unmake them: in 648 Yuknoom Ch’een II of Calakmul captured B’ajlaj Chan K’awiil of Dos Pilas and restored him as his vassal, and in 738 Copán’s king Waxaklajuun Ub’aah K’awiil was captured and beheaded by his own vassal, the ruler of <b>Quiriguá</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classic-maya">Social Hierarchy of the Classic Maya</a></li><li><blockquote>K'ak' Tiliw Chan Yopaat, ruler of the Maya city of <b>Quiriguá</b>, dedicated Stela E in its Great Plaza.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
